          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "On Thursdays the library stayed open an hour later than anyone expected. This was not advertised. The librarian simply forgot to turn the sign, and over time the town came to depend on her forgetting.\n\nA baker arrived with flour in the creases of his hands and read about deep sea fish. A girl who had recently learned to whistle came to practice not whistling. An old man took an atlas from the shelf and looked up the village where he was born. It was too small to be marked, but he liked knowing exactly where the blank space belonged.\n\nOne winter evening the power went out. Nobody moved at first. Then the baker said that some fish made their own light, though he couldn't remember how. The girl whistled once, experimentally, and the old man laughed. Without the hum of the lights, they could hear snow sliding off the roof.\n\nThe librarian found a flashlight behind the desk. It illuminated a surprisingly small circle, just enough for one person to read. She offered it to the girl, who handed it to the old man, who held it over the baker's book. They bent together to look at a fish with a lantern dangling in front of its teeth.\n\nThe lights came back at eight. By then the librarian had remembered the sign. She went to the door, turned it to CLOSED, and drew the blinds. Behind her, the baker turned another page. She went to see what he'd found.",
